* 14 Sep 26 dm  written, from the Windows port's PEER_LOCAL of 5 Sep 2026 (its
*               PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 170), for APISRVR's remote-administrator
*               gate (S.17).  One Linux addition: a Unix-socket peer is local.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call !peer_local(is.local, peer.addr)
*
*    is.local   @true  the peer is this machine
*               @false the peer is somewhere else, OR no peer address was found
*    peer.addr  the address it decided on, '' when there was none
*
*    status()  0  a peer address was found and classified
*              1  no peer address at all - a console session, or a transport
*                 that reports none
*
*    ONE RULE, KEPT IN ONE FILE, as the port's is.  Its only caller here is
*    APISRVR.  The port's second caller, LOGIN's ssh gate (its PRE_RELEASE 167),
*    does not transfer: here an administrator's ssh session is a real shell by
*    PRE_RELEASE 13, outside LOGIN, so a gate in LOGIN would stop nothing.
*
*    BOTH TRANSPORTS ARE READ, AND "else" WOULD BE THE BUG (the port's rule).
*    ssh reports the peer in SSH_CLIENT; the API reports it in system(42),
*    which is ip_addr, filled by getpeername() in linuxio.c for TCP.  A session
*    is one or the other, so reading both costs nothing.
*
*    AN EMPTY ADDRESS IS NOT LOCAL, AND THAT DIRECTION IS DELIBERATE.  An API
*    session always has a peer - it is a socket - so an empty address means
*    something is wrong, and refusing is the safe direction.
*
*    WHAT "LOCAL" MEANS IS LOOPBACK, NOT "THE SAME COMPUTER".  A connection to
*    this machine's own LAN address is NOT local: it travelled over the network
*    and anything else on the network could have sent it.
*
*    LINUX: A UNIX-SOCKET PEER IS LOCAL.  For an AF_UNIX connection linuxio.c
*    puts the socket's PATH in ip_addr (it has no address), and only a process
*    on this machine can open a Unix socket.  So an address beginning "/" is
*    this machine - the loopback case of a transport Windows does not have.
*
*    AND IT DOES NOT SEE THROUGH AN ssh TUNNEL, WHICH IS A REAL LIMIT.
*    "ssh -L" terminates on this host, so a tunnelled API connection arrives
*    from 127.0.0.1 or the socket and this answers @true for it.  That is the
*    port's stated limit and here it is also the intended way round the gate:
*    a tunnel needs the account's Linux login first.  Closing it is an sshd
*    matter (AllowTcpForwarding), not something a peer address can do.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
$flags trusted

subroutine peer_local(is.local, peer.addr)
$catalog !peer_local

$include keys.h

   set.status 0

   is.local = @false
   peer.addr = ''

*  ssh first: it is the transport the port's routine was written for.
   peer.addr = trim(field(env('SSH_CLIENT'), ' ', 1))

*  Then the API.  Not "else" - see the header.
   if peer.addr = '' then peer.addr = trim(system(42))

   if peer.addr = '' then
      set.status 1
      return
   end

   begin case
      case peer.addr[1,1] = '/'
         is.local = @true
      case peer.addr = '::1'
         is.local = @true
      case peer.addr = '::ffff:127.0.0.1'
         is.local = @true
      case field(peer.addr, '.', 1) = '127'
         is.local = @true
   end case

   set.status 0
   return

* END-CODE
